diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the ospf_enterprise_static lab found no fault consistent with the reported symptom. Phase A (L2/infrastructure) confirmed that all 15 devices have clean interface states, correct IP and gateway assignments, no duplicate MAC addresses, and no ACL or nftables rules blocking any protocol. Phase B (control plane) confirmed that all six FRR routers running OSPF are healthy: every daemon is active, every neighbor is in Full state, and all routers hold a complete 10-route OSPF table; tc_snapshot found no traffic-control shaping, rate limiting, or latency injection on any of the 30 scanned interfaces, ruling out a dns_lookup_latency or bandwidth fault. Phase D (service layer) confirmed that both host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1 correctly resolve web0.local, web1.local, web2.local, and web3.local via the single nameserver at 10.200.0.2, that HTTP requests to all four portals return HTTP 200 in approximately 7 ms, and that all four web servers answer localhost probes in 1 ms; pressure_sweep found no stress tools, no CPU spikes, and no socket floods on any device. The user's report of intermittent slowness and connectivity drops is UNCONFIRMED: there is no flap process, no tc-injected delay, no ACL mechanism, and no historical carrier or OSPF event that could produce the described on-off pattern. Root cause: no injected or active fault is present in the lab; the snapshot state is fully healthy across all layers.
Fix: No corrective action is required — the topology is operating correctly. If the user continues to experience issues, capture a timestamp and the specific error message for further investigation.
